# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Delivery outcomes — the consequences of every letter, every recipient.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ─── The Light Above the Blue Door (Theo → Mari) ─────────────────────

=== out.blue_door.mari
~ letterbox on
~ npc mari hold on
n: *Mari wipes her hands on her apron twice before she takes it. She reads it standing up. Then she reads it again, sitting down on the step.*
mari[sad]: "If the light's still on..."
mari[sad]: That idiot. That beautiful, *stupid* idiot. Two years. I stopped two years ago.
~ music sad
mari[sad]: ...
~ npc mari hold off
n: *She stands, drags a crate under the iron bracket above the blue door, and climbs it in her floury shoes.*
~ npc mari walk 1445 952
~ npc mari face right
~ npc mari anim reach
~ wait 0.6
~ sfx match
~ set bakery_lamp_lit
~ refresh
n: *The wick catches. Gold light spills down the blue door and over the cobbles, as if it had only been waiting to be asked.*
mari[happy]: There. *There* you are.
mari: Wait — wait here.
~ npc mari walk 1318 956
n: *She vanishes inside. You hear a drawer, a pencil, a sniff. She comes back with a folded square of paper.*
mari: My reply. Three words. Took me twelve years and a minute and a half. Will you post it? Bram takes the post across on the morning run.
~ item mari_reply
~ letterbox off
~ consequence "Mari lit the lamp above the blue door." "For the first time in two years, someone across the bay might see it."
~ objective "Post Mari's reply in the post office outbox."

=== out.blue_door.odile
odile[thinking]: Hm. A blue door, a lamp, cinnamon. You've been thorough, I'll give you that.
odile[happy]: But it's not mine, dear. My lamp has never once gone out — that's rather the point of me. Whoever this is for has let *theirs* go dark.
odile: And I've never had a brother or sister to quarrel with at a funeral. Look again. Look *up*.
n: *She hands the letter back with a little pat, the way you'd return a lost glove.*
~ return_letter blue_door
~ set misdelivered_once
~ objective "Find who 'The Light Above the Blue Door' was really written for."

# ─── If the Lamp Is Lit (Odile → Tobias) ─────────────────────────────

=== out.lamp_lit.tobias
~ letterbox on
~ npc tobias hold on
n: *Tobias reads the letter once, very slowly, holding it out at arm's length the way old men do.*
n: *Then he sits down on the lighthouse step, as if his legs have simply resigned.*
tobias[sad]: "If you light the lamp tonight..."
tobias[sad]: I lit it. Of course I lit it. I lit it every night of my life.
tobias: I never knew there was a question.
~ music sad
tobias[sad]: Thirteenth of October. The night before the wedding. She was down there on the pier. Watching my light. Thinking it was my answer.
tobias[angry]: Forty-one years she's let me think she simply didn't care for me. Forty-one *years*, Odile Vance.
n: *He laughs — a short, cracked, astonished sound, like ice breaking on a pond.*
~ npc tobias hold off
tobias: ...Lantern Night. Hm. The anniversary. The pier.
tobias: The lamp can manage without me for one night. Just one. After forty-one years, I think it's owed.
[if $odile_asked_keep] n: *You think of Odile's shaking hand, held out for the letter. You hope you've done right.*
~ set tobias_knows
[if not has_item("lighthouse_key")] tobias: Here. Take this. If I'm going to be standing on a pier, somebody else had better have a key to the lamp.
[if not has_item("lighthouse_key")] ~ item lighthouse_key
~ arrive ps_1140
~ letterbox off
~ ripple
~ consequence "Tobias knows." "On Lantern Night, at 11:40, he'll be waiting at the end of the pier."
~ objective "The Chute is ringing at the post office."

=== out.lamp_lit.odile
odile[sad]: Thank you, dear. For bringing it to me, and not to him.
n: *She opens the stove door and holds the letter over the coals. For a long moment, she doesn't let go.*
~ music sad
n: *Then she does.*
~ sfx fire
odile: There. Forty-one years, and it went up in four seconds. Paper's like that.
odile[happy]: Now. Tea. And we'll never speak of this again. I've had a great deal of practice at that.
~ set odile_burned
~ consequence "Odile burned her letter." "Some letters stay unsent. That is its own kind of answer."

# ─── The Far Shore (future June → June) ──────────────────────────────

=== out.far_shore.june
~ letterbox on
~ npc june hold on
n: *June reads it twice by the little brass lamp on her telescope. The second time, her lips move.*
june[surprised]: "We got on the ferry." We... I made it. I *make* it.
june[happy]: A comet! I named a comet! It's grumpy! Of *course* it's grumpy!
n: *Then she reaches the third paragraph, and goes quiet.*
june[sad]: "Grandpa's lamp went dark the winter after we left."
june[sad]: I never climbed it with him. Not once.
~ music sad
june: He's never told me about any stars he named for Grandma. He never tells me anything. He just lights the lamp and looks at the sea like it owes him money.
~ npc june hold off
june[thinking]: Okay. Okay. New plan. I'm still going. But before I go, I'm climbing those stupid stairs. All hundred and twelve of them. With him.
~ fade_letter lamp_room
~ set june_path "leave"
~ set june_climbed
~ letterbox off
~ ripple
~ consequence "June will still take the ferry — but first, she'll climb the lighthouse with her grandfather." "Somewhere in 2046, a letter written by lamplight quietly unwrites itself."

=== out.far_shore.felix
felix[thinking]: "Dear Stargazer." Well, that's obviously — and then "Grandpa's lamp"? My grandfather hated lamps.
felix[surprised]: Twenty forty-six! A letter from twenty years hence! Do you understand what this means? *Evidence*, Wren! Physical evidence!
n: *Before you can stop him, he's sealed it in a glass specimen case with a brass label: EXHIBIT A.*
felix: I'll return it when my study is complete. Ten years. Fifteen at most.
~ set felix_has_letter
~ consequence "Felix sealed the letter in a glass case." "It will never reach the stargazer it was written for."

# ─── A Hundred and Twelve Stairs (future June → June) ───────────────

=== out.lamp_room.june
~ letterbox on
~ npc june hold on
n: *June reads the logbook page standing very still, the wind tugging at her yellow coat.*
june[sad]: "I'm the keeper now."
june[angry]: No. No, no. That's — I'd never. I'd *never*.
june[sad]: "I've never seen a sunrise. Not a real one."
~ music sad
n: *She sits down hard on the boards. For a long time, she watches the thin gold line at the edge of the sea.*
june[sad]: "Don't do what I did and stay with half of it."
june: ...
~ npc june hold off
june[thinking]: She's not telling me to stay. She's telling me — if I stay, I have to actually *stay*. Not just... not leave.
n: *She takes a ticket out of her coat pocket. Turns it over. Holds it out to you.*
june: Hold onto this for me? If I'm staying, I'm doing it properly. Whole heart.
june[happy]: Grandpa's going to be *insufferable* about this.
~ item ferry_ticket
~ fade_letter far_shore
~ set june_path "stay"
~ letterbox off
~ ripple
~ consequence "June gave you her ferry ticket." "Somewhere in 2046, a research ship sails south without her — and a lamp is lit with a whole heart."

=== out.lamp_room.felix
felix[thinking]: "I'm the keeper now"? Of what? I keep *time*, not lamps. A hundred and twelve stairs — the tower only has ninety.
felix[surprised]: But the date! Twenty forty-six! Wren, this is extraordinary. I must study it.
n: *He seals it in a glass specimen case with a brass label: EXHIBIT B.*
~ set felix_has_letter
~ consequence "Felix sealed the letter in a glass case." "It will never reach the stargazer it was written for."

# ─── The Lady with the Green Umbrella (Bram → Mari) ──────────────────

=== out.umbrella.mari
~ letterbox on
~ npc mari hold on
n: *Mari reads it, and frowns, and reads it again, and then claps a floury hand over her mouth.*
mari[surprised]: "The only weather I look forward to"?
mari[happy]: "Evry Thursday"? "Wether"? Oh, there's only one man alive who spells like that.
mari[happy]: *Bram Calloway.*
~ music town
n: *She laughs until she has to sit down on a flour sack. Then she stops laughing, rather suddenly, and looks at the letter for a long time.*
mari[shy]: Thirty-one years at that wheel. And he's been looking back at the stern. At *me*.
~ npc mari hold off
mari: The old walrus. The great, soft, ridiculous walrus.
mari[thinking]: Thursday's the day after Lantern Night. I think... I think I might sit at the bow this week.
~ set mari_bow
~ letterbox off
~ consequence "Mari knows who the man at the wheel is." "This Thursday, she'll sit at the bow."

=== out.umbrella.odile
odile[surprised]: For me? "The lady with the green umbrella"...
odile[happy]: Oh, how *lovely*. I haven't had a love letter since the Coronation.
odile[thinking]: I haven't ridden the 6:10 in seventeen years, of course. Somebody's got muddled. But I shan't tell him. Let the poor man wonder.
n: *She tucks it into her shawl with a smile that you suspect will be very confusing for someone, come Thursday.*
~ set odile_kept_umbrella
~ consequence "Odile kept the letter, delighted." "On the 6:10, a man at the wheel will never know why the postmistress winks at him."

=== out.umbrella.bram
bram[shy]: Oh. Oh no. You found me out.
bram[sad]: I wrote it in April. And May. And June, and July. I keep it in my coat. Every Thursday I think, today, Bram. Today. And every Thursday she feeds the gulls, and I steer the boat.
bram: Give it here. I'll... I'll give it her myself. Someday.
~ set bram_kept_letter
~ consequence "Bram took his letter back." "Someday, he says."

# ─── Eleven Fifty-Two (Felix → Daniel) ──────────────────────────────

=== out.eleven_fifty_two.tobias
~ letterbox on
~ npc tobias hold on
n: *Tobias reads it standing. His thumb moves over one line, again and again: "Everyone's allowed to be afraid once, Fe."*
tobias[sad]: That's Daniel. That's my boy, that is. That's exactly how he talked.
tobias: Felix wrote this. That night. From up the tower.
tobias[angry]: Seventeen years he's carried this. Seventeen years thinking I'd curse him for it.
~ npc tobias hold off
tobias[sad]: ...Come with me.
~ fade out 0.8
~ goto_location square felix_scene
~ npc tobias show 520 960
~ npc tobias face left
~ npc felix face right
~ fade in 0.8
n: *Felix is at his bench when Tobias walks in without knocking. He sees the letter in the old man's hand and goes the colour of candle wax.*
felix[sad]: Tobias. I—
tobias: He knew, Felix. Daniel knew. He came by the cottage at nine and he told me. "Felix is scared, Da. Don't let him know I know."
tobias[sad]: He went because he wanted you safe. Because he loved you, you daft old cog.
felix[sad]: I should have been on that boat.
tobias[sad]: And I should have been awake. And the gears should have been dry. And the sky should have been kind.
tobias: The lighthouse was my fault, Felix. Not yours.
felix[sad]: The lighthouse wasn't yours either.
~ music hope
n: *For a long while, two old men sit in a clock shop and say nothing at all. It is the best conversation either of them has had in seventeen years.*
n: *Then Felix stands, climbs the tower stairs, and does something to the escapement with a tiny brass key.*
~ sfx clock_start
~ set clock_running
~ refresh
~ wait 1.0
n: *Above Lantern Square, for the first time in seventeen years, the town clock ticks. 11:53.*
~ letterbox off
~ ripple
~ consequence "The town clock is running again." "11:53 — seventeen years late, and exactly on time."

=== out.eleven_fifty_two.june
~ letterbox on
~ npc june hold on
n: *June reads it slowly, mouthing the words. When she reaches the line in quotation marks, she stops.*
june[sad]: "Everyone's allowed to be afraid once, Fe."
june[sad]: That's him. That's Dad. I've never heard him say *anything*. I've got one photo and his name on a stone. And now I've got a sentence.
~ music sad
n: *She folds it very carefully into her inside pocket, next to her heart, the way you'd carry something alive.*
~ npc june hold off
june[thinking]: Felix wrote this. He's been up that tower for seventeen years thinking it was his fault.
june: I'm going to go and tell him thank you. For giving me a sentence.
~ fade out 0.8
~ wait 0.6
~ fade in 0.8
n: *That night, Felix climbed the clock tower and turned a tiny brass key.*
~ sfx clock_start
~ set clock_running
~ set clock_backward
~ refresh
n: *The town clock started again — backwards. Felix says it's a calibration issue. June says it's her dad, being funny.*
~ letterbox off
~ ripple
~ consequence "The town clock is running — backwards." "June carries a sentence of her father's next to her heart."

=== out.eleven_fifty_two.mari
~ letterbox on
~ npc mari hold on
n: *Mari reads it in the bakery doorway. She reads it twice. Her face does nothing at all.*
mari[sad]: He knew. Of course he knew. Daniel always knew when someone was frightened. He'd go and stand next to them like a wall.
mari[angry]: Felix let him. Felix let him go out there and—
mari[sad]: No. No. That's not fair. Daniel chose. Daniel always chose.
~ npc mari hold off
n: *She folds the letter into her apron pocket.*
mari: I'll talk to Felix. Not tonight. But I will.
~ set mari_knows_felix
~ letterbox off
~ consequence "Mari knows the truth about the Marguerite." "She'll talk to Felix. Someday. Not tonight."

=== out.eleven_fifty_two.memorial
~ letterbox on
n: *You kneel at the memorial stone and tuck the letter beneath the jar of sea-lavender at its foot.*
n: *DANIEL FENN, the stone says. Beloved son, husband, father.*
~ music sad
n: *The wind picks at a corner of the envelope. By morning it has carried the letter out over the water — toward wherever the Marguerite went.*
~ set letter_to_sea
~ letterbox off
~ consequence "Felix's letter went out with the tide, to Daniel." "Felix will never know it was read."

=== out.eleven_fifty_two.felix
felix[surprised]: Where did you — no. No, that's mine. That's—
n: *He snatches it, reads the first line, and goes white.*
felix[sad]: I wrote this. That night. I didn't know where to send it, so I didn't send it anywhere.
felix[angry]: Some letters should stay unsent, Wren.
n: *He locks it in a drawer, turns the little key, and goes back to the stopped clock as if you had never come in.*
~ set felix_locked
~ consequence "Felix locked his letter away." "The town clock still reads 11:52."

# ─── Little Bird (Mari → June) ──────────────────────────────────────

=== out.little_bird.june
~ letterbox on
~ npc june hold on
n: *June reads it, and her face crumples and uncrumples like paper in a fist.*
june[sad]: She knew. She *knew*, about the ticket. Since Tuesday.
june[sad]: "Let me pack you bread for the journey. Let me wave."
~ music sad
june[angry]: Why does she have to be so — so — *Mum* about everything.
~ npc june hold off
n: *She wipes her face hard on a yellow sleeve.*
june: I'm going to go and help her with the lantern loaves. Don't follow me. Don't tell anyone I was crying. I wasn't. It's the wind.
~ set june_mum
~ letterbox off
~ consequence "June and Mari are baking the Lantern Night loaves together." "Whatever June decides, she won't leave in silence."

=== out.little_bird.wren
n: *You fold the letter and tuck it into your own coat, near your heart.*
n: *"My little bird." It's silly. It isn't for you. But you keep it anyway — it is very difficult to give away a letter like that, once you've read it.*
~ set kept_little_bird
~ consequence "You kept a letter that wasn't yours." "In the bakery, the words go unsaid."

# ─── Letters from changed futures (addressed to you) ────────────────

=== out.ps_1140
~ set read_ps_1140
~ consequence "A warning from 2031." "On Lantern Night, someone must be in the lamp room at 11:40."
[if lamp_keeper() == "nobody" and not has_item("lighthouse_key")] ~ objective "On Lantern Night, someone must be in the lamp room by 11:40."

=== out.ticking
~ set read_ticking
~ consequence "A letter from a future where the clock never stopped again." "Twenty years without a second lost."

=== out.second_oven
~ set read_second_oven
~ consequence "A letter from a future where Theo came home." "The lamp above the blue door hasn't gone out in twenty years."
